::ENG4_U02_L06_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.R.11] Classic context: In Homer's The Odyssey, Odysseus spends years trying to return home after war. The epic uses a journey pattern involving obstacles, temptation, disguise, recognition, and the question of what home means after long absence. Contemporary passage: After three years working overseas, Amara landed before dawn in the city where she had grown up. She expected the streets to recognize her, but new towers had risen where the market used to lean into the river. At her mother's apartment, the lock had been changed for safety, and Amara stood in the hallway with a suitcase and a passport full of stamps. She almost laughed at the old story she had loved as a child, the one where the wanderer returned and the house knew him. Her mother opened the door, studied her face, and said, "You have brought the distance with you." Only then did Amara understand that coming home was not the end of the journey but the last test of it. Which classic influence is clearest? {
~A. A courtroom trial#This is not central.
=B. The homecoming journey after long absence#Correct. Both texts center return and recognition.
~C. A harvest festival#This is not present.
~D. A battle scene#The modern passage is not about battle.
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.11] Classic context: The Odyssey asks what home means after long absence. Contemporary passage: Amara expected the streets to recognize her, but new towers had risen where the market used to lean into the river. What does the contemporary passage transform? {
~A. The classic pattern becomes a cooking lesson.#This is unrelated.
~B. The journey becomes a literal sea voyage only.#The modern passage is urban.
~C. The homecoming becomes meaningless.#The ending gives it meaning.
=D. The return home becomes a confrontation with change rather than simple restoration.#Correct
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Contemporary passage: Amara stood in the hallway with a suitcase and a passport full of stamps. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
=A. Amara arrives carrying evidence of extended travel.#Correct
~B. Amara has never left home.#This contradicts the passage.
~C. Amara loses her passport in the market.#This is not stated.
~D. Amara refuses to enter the building because she dislikes doors.#This is not stated.
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] In literary analysis, transformation means: {
~A. A later text repeats every detail exactly.#That is copying, not transformation.
~B. A text removes all meaning.#This is not the term.
=C. A later text changes a source pattern to create new meaning.#Correct
~D. A character changes clothes only.#This is too narrow.
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.R.01] Contemporary passage: "You have brought the distance with you." What is the functional significance of this line? {
~A. It gives a measurement in miles.#This is too literal.
~B. It means Amara brought a map.#This is not stated.
=C. It reveals that travel has changed Amara internally.#Correct
~D. It ends the theme of homecoming.#It deepens the theme.
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_Q06::[MLA.ENG4.R.11] Classic context: The Odyssey includes recognition after long absence. Contemporary passage: Her mother opened the door and studied Amara's face. Which classic pattern is echoed? {
=A. Recognition after return#Correct
~B. A formal battle challenge#No battle occurs.
~C. A sea monster episode#No monster appears.
~D. A harvest song#No harvest appears.
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_Q07::[MLA.ENG4.R.11] How is the contemporary passage different from the classic context? {
~A. It removes homecoming completely.#Homecoming is central.
~B. It retells every event from The Odyssey.#It does not.
~C. It focuses only on war.#It focuses on return after work overseas.
=D. It shifts the homecoming test from external adventure to inner identity.#Correct
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_Q08::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Classic influence is best shown when a response: {
~A. Says two texts are exactly the same.#That misses transformation.
=B. Explains both a shared pattern and the later text's change to that pattern.#Correct
~C. Ignores the classic text.#Then influence cannot be shown.
~D. Lists character names only.#That is insufficient.
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_Q09::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Classic context: Odysseus spends years trying to return home after war. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Odysseus never wants to go home.#This contradicts the context.
~B. Odysseus is a modern office worker.#This is not in the classic context.
=C. Odysseus has a long, difficult return home after war.#Correct
~D. Odysseus finishes his trip in one day.#This contradicts years.
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_Q10::[MLA.ENG4.R.01] Contemporary passage: new towers had risen where the market used to lean into the river. What is the function of this setting detail? {
=A. It shows home has changed during Amara's absence.#Correct
~B. It proves the city is ancient Greece.#This is not supported.
~C. It removes the theme of return.#It supports the theme.
~D. It says Amara never knew the city.#She grew up there.
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_Q11::[MLA.ENG4.R.11] Which statement best analyzes influence? {
~A. The modern text is unrelated because it has no ships.#Influence can transform setting.
~B. The modern text copies every event from the classic.#It does not.
~C. The classic context should be ignored.#The standard asks for influence.
=D. The modern text uses the classic homecoming pattern but changes it into a meditation on altered identity.#Correct
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_Q12::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] What does contemporary mean in this lesson? {
~A. Ancient only#This is the opposite.
=B. Belonging to the present or recent time#Correct
~C. Written without characters#This is not the meaning.
~D. Impossible to compare#This is not the meaning.
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_Q13::[MLA.ENG4.R.11] Contemporary passage: the old story she had loved as a child, the one where the wanderer returned and the house knew him. What does this allusion do? {
~A. It removes the classic influence.#It emphasizes influence.
=B. It signals the classic homecoming pattern directly.#Correct
~C. It proves Amara is Odysseus.#It does not make her literally Odysseus.
~D. It changes the passage into nonfiction.#It remains literary.
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_Q14::[MLA.ENG4.R.01] Contemporary passage: the lock had been changed for safety. What does this detail add? {
~A. Amara's mother has moved away forever.#She opens the door.
~B. The apartment no longer exists.#It exists.
=C. Home is familiar but not exactly accessible in the same way.#Correct
~D. The journey was imaginary.#This is not supported.
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_Q15::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Contemporary passage: Only then did Amara understand that coming home was not the end of the journey but the last test of it. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
=A. Amara realizes that returning home creates one final challenge.#Correct
~B. Amara realizes the journey ended before she traveled.#This is illogical.
~C. Amara believes homecoming is easy and complete.#This contradicts last test.
~D. Amara decides the journey never happened.#This is not stated.
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_Q16::[MLA.ENG4.R.11] Which feature connects both the classic context and contemporary passage? {
~A. A digital archive#This belongs to a different lesson.
~B. A storm-damaged city#This belongs to Lesson 5.
~C. A council vote#This belongs to Lesson 2.
=D. Return after long absence#Correct
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_Q17::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] An allusion is: {
~A. A list of definitions#This is not allusion.
~B. A grammar error#This is unrelated.
=C. A reference to another text, person, event, or idea#Correct
~D. A literal passport stamp#This is not allusion.
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_Q18::[MLA.ENG4.R.11] Contemporary passage: She expected the streets to recognize her. How does this transform the classic recognition pattern? {
=A. Recognition shifts from people recognizing a hero to a place failing to feel familiar.#Correct
~B. Recognition disappears from the passage.#The expectation remains.
~C. Recognition means reading a passport only.#This is too narrow.
~D. Recognition becomes a battle.#No battle appears.
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_Q19::[MLA.ENG4.R.01] Contemporary passage: a passport full of stamps. What is the functional significance of this detail? {
~A. It proves Amara cannot travel.#The stamps show travel.
=B. It represents distance, travel, and accumulated experience.#Correct
~C. It means the passage is about stamp collecting.#That is too literal.
~D. It removes the homecoming pattern.#It supports it.
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_Q20::[MLA.ENG4.R.11] Which response avoids overclaiming influence? {
~A. The contemporary passage is exactly The Odyssey.#This overclaims.
~B. The contemporary passage has no relationship to classic literature because it is modern.#This ignores influence.
~C. The Odyssey is about passports.#This is false.
=D. The contemporary passage echoes The Odyssey's homecoming pattern while changing the setting, stakes, and meaning.#Correct
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_Q21::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Contemporary passage: She expected the streets to recognize her, but new towers had risen where the market used to lean into the river. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
=A. Amara expected familiarity, but the city had changed while she was away.#Correct
~B. Amara found the market unchanged.#This contradicts the passage.
~C. Amara had never seen the city before.#She grew up there.
~D. Amara built the new towers.#This is not stated.
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_Q22::[MLA.ENG4.R.11] Classic context: homecoming asks what home means after absence. Contemporary passage: Amara's mother says, "You have brought the distance with you." What is the best analysis? {
~A. The modern text says distance is only geographic.#The line makes it internal.
~B. The modern text rejects all classic influence.#It alludes to homecoming.
=C. The modern text transforms homecoming into a question of internal change.#Correct
~D. The modern text is about weather.#It is not.
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_Q23::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] What does psychological mean in the phrase psychological test? {
~A. Related only to boat travel#This is unrelated.
~B. Related to punctuation#This is unrelated.
~C. Related to cooking#This is unrelated.
=D. Related to the mind, identity, or inner experience#Correct
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_Q24::[MLA.ENG4.R.01] Contemporary passage: Her mother opened the door, studied her face. What is the function of this action? {
~A. It proves Amara is invisible.#This is not stated.
=B. It creates a moment of recognition and uncertainty.#Correct
~C. It removes the mother from the scene.#The mother is active.
~D. It ends the theme of change.#It deepens the theme.
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_Q25::[MLA.ENG4.R.11] What is the strongest final evaluation of the contemporary passage's relationship to the classic? {
=A. It uses the classic homecoming journey to explore modern questions about changed places, changed selves, and belonging.#Correct
~B. It copies the classic exactly because both include travel.#This ignores transformation.
~C. It has no classic influence because it is set in a city.#Influence can shift setting.
~D. It only explains passport rules.#The passport is a literary detail.
}
